  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1132 AM MST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nGOES satellite imagery shows a few cirrus clouds moving south out of \nWyoming in the northerly flow aloft. Other than that, it will be \nsunny, dry, and warm across the area with generally light winds. \nHighs should reach the mid 60s across the plains and I-25 corridor\nwith around 50 degF in the mountain valleys and east slope \nfoothills. Tonight's lows will be seasonally cool but warmer than \naverage for early November with mostly clear skies and light \nwinds. Looking at 30s across the plains and 20s in the mountain \nvalleys.\n\nA positively tilted ridge builds over Colorado Sunday through \nTuesday. It will remain dry with above average temperatures. \nEnsemble temperature spreads from any ensemble forecast system are\nvery small, resulting in high confidence in the expected \ntemperatures across our area. An example is REFS for Monday has at\nmost a 3-4 degree difference between the 10th/90th percentile \nmaximum temperature at just a few northeast plains locations. The \nspread is less than that elsewhere. Needless to say temperatures \nacross the plains will be 65-70 deg each afternoon. The mountain \nvalleys/east slope foothills will likewise be warm with highs in \nthe low to mid 50s.\n\nLate on Tuesday models have a weak shortwave trough moving across \nthe northern Rockies. GFS/GEFS are generally outliers with a colder \nand further south trough than any other modeling system. However, \nconfidence overall is increasing that northwest flow aloft \nassociated with the trough to the northeast will advect cooler air\ninto our area especially by Wednesday morning, including a \nsurface cold front. Ensemble guidance has essentially zero \nchance of precipitation with the trough/front passage across the \nplains, and it looks really dry aloft too such that for now we \nhave keeping PoPs in the mountains below 10%, though that could \ncertainly change as we get closer to Tuesday. West flow and cooler\nair associated with a trough is usually all it takes for flurries\nacross the highest peaks/ridges even if models look too dry. \nTemps area-wide on Wednesday are likely to be cooler to some \nextent given the front and proximity of the trough, for now 4-6 \ndegrees cooler is in the forecast. It's also likely to be breezy \nacross the northern tier counties of Colorado later Tuesday into \nearly Wednesday with northwest winds 15-20 mph gusting up to 30 \nmph. For now, it's looks cool enough that RH should not drop to \nred flag criteria. Thursday will also be breezy across the far \neastern plains, but it does not look like we'll have any fire \nweather issues.\n\nThursday and through the weekend the ridge rebuilds over Colorado \nand does not budge at all, resulting in very little uncertainty in \ntemperatures and precipitation chances through the weekend. It looks \ndry across our area with temperatures continuing to be 8-13 degrees \nabove normal each afternoon.",
